-- title:
--   Minimal primes over q at a crossing point are the two branch primes
-- statement:
--   Fix $N_0 \ge 1$ and a prime $q$ with $q \nmid N_0$, and let $\mathfrak X$ be a term of the structure `DRModelPackageLevel N₀ q hqN`, i.e. a package consisting of: the proper, flat, locally finitely presented and integral model `X N₀ q` over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of `DRLevel.R q` with integrally closed sections on affine opens; a smooth, geometrically integral generic fibre; a curve model `Meta` of the modular function field of level $N_0q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ identified with the geometric fibre, compatible with the Galois action and pinned by the Laurent-series expansions of the chart algebra; and distinguished sections $\varepsilon_\infty$, $\varepsilon_0$ over the base, among further data. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation domain with a ring map $\rho_O$ from `DRLevel.R q` whose maximal ideal is $(q)$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$ with a ring map $O \to \kappa$, assume the scheme `DRLevel.XO ρO` integral, and let $x$ be a point of it whose stalk $A = \mathcal O_{x}$ is Noetherian. Suppose both points `𝔛.ξinf ρO toκ` and `𝔛.ξzero ρO toκ` specialise to $x$, and let $\mathfrak p_1, \mathfrak p_2 \subseteq A$ be the contractions of the maximal ideals of the stalks at these two points along the corresponding specialisation maps out of $A$. The conclusion asserts: $\mathfrak p_1$ and $\mathfrak p_2$ are prime; the image of $q$ in $A$ lies in each of them; every prime of $A$ minimal over the ideal $(q)$ equals $\mathfrak p_1$ or $\mathfrak p_2$; and every prime of $A$ of height $1$ containing $q$ equals $\mathfrak p_1$ or $\mathfrak p_2$.
--
--   This is the local statement at a crossing point of the special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(N_0q)$ over a discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $q$: the only height-one primes of the local ring containing $q$ are the two branch primes coming from the generic points of the two components through $x$. It is used in the computation identifying the intersection of the two branch ideals with the ideal generated by the germ of $q$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_DRModelPackageLevel_eq_comap_or_eq_comap_of_mem_minimalPrimes_natCast_of_specializes.lean

